    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-49150",
      "knowledge_point_id": "8e6b5d85-0b53-580f-9520-6c33463231d5",
      "knowledge_point": "Integrating Using Inverse Trigonometric Functions and Some Manipulations",
      "problem": "$\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}-7}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$",
      "worked_solution": "This integral doesn't look like one of the basic ones. However, we can manipulate the numerator a bit  to make it look more like the denominator, which reveals a more familiar expression: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-7}{{x}^{2}+1} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+1-1-7}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)-8}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{x}^{2}+1}}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}+1}}-\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemembering that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C,\n$$\n\nwe can now calculate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)=\\int {f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (1-\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}+1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{8}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\text{d}x-8\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x-8\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
